<h2>Why Lead Paint Is Still a Real Concern in Western Massachusetts</h2><p>Homes built before 1978 are everywhere in the Connecticut River Valley. Walk through the older neighborhoods of Springfield, Holyoke, or Westfield and you will find beautiful Victorian and Colonial-era houses with layers of history — and layers of paint — built up over decades. That history matters, because paint manufactured before 1978 often contained lead, a neurotoxin that is harmless when sealed and intact but dangerous when it is sanded, scraped, or disturbed.</p><p>According to <a href="https://www.epa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">the EPA</a>, approximately 87 percent of homes built before 1940 contain some lead-based paint. The number drops for homes built between 1940 and 1978, but it is still significant. If your house falls into either window, you should treat lead paint as a real possibility rather than a remote one before you schedule any exterior work.</p><h2>How to Test Before You Paint</h2><figure class="blog-figure"><img src="/images/stock/web/blog-lead-paint-and-older-homes-what-to-know-before-repainting-in-massachusetts-2.jpg" alt="Exterior siding mid-repaint on a New England home" width="1100" height="619" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></figure><p>Testing is the first practical step, and it is not complicated. You have two reliable options.</p><h3>DIY Test Kits</h3><p>Swab-style test kits are available at most hardware stores and can give you a quick answer on a specific surface. They are inexpensive and useful for a spot check, but they can produce false negatives on certain paint colors and do not give you a complete picture of the whole exterior.</p><h3>Professional Lead Inspection or Risk Assessment</h3><p>A Massachusetts-licensed lead inspector uses an XRF (X-ray fluorescence) analyzer or takes paint chip samples sent to a certified lab. This approach covers the entire home and produces a written report you can share with your contractor. If you are selling the property, this documentation also satisfies the state's disclosure requirements. For older homes in areas like Agawam or West Springfield where the housing stock skews pre-1950, a full professional inspection is money well spent before any repainting project begins.</p><h2>What Massachusetts Law Requires</h2><p>Massachusetts has some of the strictest lead paint laws in the country, and they layer on top of federal rules. Here is what matters most for homeowners planning exterior painting.</p><h3>The Federal RRP Rule</h3><p>Any contractor you hire to work on a pre-1978 home must follow the EPA's Renovation, Repair, and Painting (RRP) Rule. <a href="https://www.epa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">the EPA</a> requires that contractors be certified and follow specific work practices — containment of dust and debris, careful cleanup, and proper disposal — when disturbing more than six square feet of painted surface per room on the interior or more than twenty square feet on the exterior. Almost any full exterior repaint will cross that threshold.</p><h3>Massachusetts Deleading Law</h3><p>The state goes further. If a child under six lives in the home, Massachusetts law requires that lead hazards be corrected, not simply managed. This applies to rental properties and owner-occupied homes alike. Even if you are not legally required to delead, choosing an RRP-certified contractor protects everyone on the job site and reduces your liability if a hazard is later discovered.</p><h3>What to Ask Your Contractor</h3><p>Before signing anything, ask to see the contractor's EPA RRP certification number and confirm they carry appropriate insurance. A reputable contractor will not hesitate to provide this. You can learn more about what to expect from a certified exterior painting project at <a href="/services.html#exterior-painting">our exterior painting service</a>.</p><div class="blog-cta-inline"><p>Would you rather we handled it? Estimates are always free.</p><a class="btn btn--gold" href="/contact.html#estimate-form">Get a Free Estimate</a></div><h2>Safe Work Practices During an Exterior Repaint</h2><p>Even when you hire the right people, understanding what good work looks like helps you stay informed throughout the project.</p><h3>Containment and Cleanup</h3><p>Certified crews are required to set up plastic sheeting around the work area to catch paint chips and dust, especially important when scraping old siding. In New England, spring and early fall are the most common times for exterior painting — the mild temperatures are ideal for paint adhesion, but wind can scatter debris quickly. A properly set-up containment area accounts for this.</p><h3>Wet Scraping and Low-Dust Tools</h3><p>Rather than dry sanding, which sends lead particles into the air, certified contractors use wet scraping techniques or HEPA-equipped power tools that capture dust at the source. the CDC notes that lead dust is the primary exposure pathway for children, making dust control the single most important protective measure on any job site near a residence.</p><h3>Disposal</h3><p>Paint chips, plastic sheeting, and disposable protective gear must be bagged and disposed of properly. In Massachusetts, residential lead paint waste is typically handled as solid waste in sealed bags, but your contractor should confirm compliance with current local requirements. Municipalities like Holyoke and Springfield may have specific guidance for bulky renovation debris.</p><h2>Cost Considerations and What Affects Pricing</h2><p>Homeowners sometimes worry that lead-safe practices add enormous cost. In reality, for most exterior repaints, the additional expense is modest — perhaps ten to twenty percent over a standard project — and it covers real value: proper containment setup, certified labor, appropriate disposal, and documentation that protects you as the homeowner.</p><p>What drives cost more than anything else is the condition of the existing paint. If your siding has widespread peeling or alligatoring, the prep work is more intensive regardless of lead content. Homes with original wood clapboard siding, common throughout Southampton and the older sections of West Springfield, often need more careful surface preparation than aluminum or vinyl-clad homes. Getting a detailed written estimate that breaks out prep, priming, and finish coats will give you a clear comparison when you evaluate proposals.</p><section class="blog-faq"><h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Do I have to remove all lead paint before repainting my home?</h3><p>No, full removal is not always required. If the existing paint is intact and stable, encapsulation — applying a specially formulated coating over the old paint — is an accepted approach under both state and federal guidelines. Full removal, called deleading, is typically required only when the paint is deteriorating or when Massachusetts law mandates it due to the presence of young children in the home.</p><h3>Can I do my own exterior prep work if lead paint is present?</h3><p>Homeowners are generally exempt from the federal RRP Rule when working on their own residence, but Massachusetts state law imposes additional restrictions. If a child under six lives in your home, disturbing lead paint without following safe work practices creates legal and health risks. Even if you are legally permitted to do the work yourself, hiring a certified contractor eliminates uncertainty and protects your family.</p><h3>How long does lead paint testing take?</h3><p>An XRF inspection by a licensed Massachusetts inspector typically takes two to four hours for a full exterior assessment, and you usually receive a written report within a few days. Lab-based paint chip sampling takes a bit longer due to processing time but is equally reliable and sometimes less expensive upfront.</p><h3>Will lead-safe painting affect my project timeline?</h3><p>In most cases, the added time is minimal. Containment setup and careful cleanup add a half-day to a day on a typical project. The bigger timeline factor in Western Massachusetts is weather — crews cannot apply most exterior paints when temperatures drop below fifty degrees Fahrenheit, which limits the working season to roughly late April through October.</p><h3>What documentation should I keep after the project is complete?</h3><p>Ask your contractor for a copy of their RRP certification, the signed renovation record (which the EPA requires contractors to keep), and any test results from pre-project sampling. Keep these documents with your home improvement records because they are valuable for insurance purposes and will likely be requested if you sell the home.</p></section>
